rm - a Unix-style delete command for AmigaDOS
=============================================

WHAT IT IS

  rm deletes files and (with -r) directory trees, like Unix rm, with
  AmigaDOS manners where the two worlds differ. Written in Amiga E
  (rm.e); install the included binary as C:rm.

  Requires Kickstart 2.04 or newer.

USAGE

  rm [-rfv] FILE | PATTERN ...

    rm old.txt
    rm #?.bak                       (pattern)
    rm -r work:tmpdir               (directory and contents)
    rm -f locked.dat                (strip delete protection first)
    rm -rv ram:t                    (say what goes as it goes)

  Flags are bundled Unix-style -- -r, -f, -v or any mix -- matching
  ls/cp/mv/mkdir in this set; `rm ?` prints usage.

  Directories need -r, like Unix. The tree is deleted contents-first,
  deepest directory first, and every directory is fully scanned
  before anything inside it goes.

  -f is force, the Amiga way: a delete-protected file (the d bit) is
  skipped and listed by default; with -f the protection is stripped
  and the delete retried. A file another task holds open always
  refuses -- -f cannot unlock it. -f also keeps rm quiet when an
  argument matches nothing.

  Volume roots are refused unconditionally: `rm -rf dh0:` answers
  "refusing to remove a volume root" and does nothing. No flag
  overrides that in this version.

  Links -- soft links, and hard links to directories -- are deleted
  as LINKS: the entry goes, the target stays, and -r never follows
  one into a cycle.

  -v lists each object as it is deleted. Ctrl-C is honoured between
  entries. Errors on one item are reported and the rest of the batch
  still runs.

  Return code: 0 clean, 5 something was skipped (listed under "not
  deleted:"), 10 some item failed, 20 break.

ABOUT

  Part of a small family of Unix-style file tools for AmigaDOS
  (ls, cp, mv, mkdir, rm), written in Amiga E:

    https://github.com/creep-ltx/AmigaTools
